.TH PTY_REGION_COMPARE 3 2026-10-03 "libptytest 0.1" "libptytest Manual"
.SH NAME
pty_region_compare, pty_screen_compare \- compare a rectangle of the
screen, or all of it, cell for cell, and explain the difference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <ptytest.h>
.PP
.B typedef struct {
.BR "    int row, col;" "           /* top-left cell, 0-based */"
.BR "    int rows, cols;" "         /* 0 or less: to the screen edge */"
.BR "    const char *label;" "      /* name in reports, or NULL */"
.BR "    const char *wildcard;" "   /* matches any one cell, or NULL */"
.BR "    int context;" "            /* 0: 2 rows; negative: none */"
.B } PtyRegion;
.PP
.BI "#define PTY_REGION(" r ", " c ", " h ", " w ")"
.B #define PTY_REGION_FULL
.PP
.BI "int pty_region_compare(PtySession *" s ", const PtyRegion *" reg ,
.BI "                       const char *const " expected "[],"
.BI "                       char *" report ", size_t " report_size );
.BI "int pty_screen_compare(PtySession *" s ", const char *const " expected "[],"
.BI "                       char *" report ", size_t " report_size );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR pty_region_compare ()
compares the rectangle
.I reg
of the screen with an expected picture:
.I expected
is a NULL-terminated array of UTF-8 strings, row
.I i
of it standing for screen row
.IR reg\->row " + " i .
Rows past the end of the array must be blank, so a stray line in the
region is a difference.
Cells outside the region are not looked at: a test can check a status
bar, a dialog or a list without restating the rest of the screen.
.I reg
may be NULL for the whole screen, which is what
.BR pty_screen_compare ()
does.
.PP
Every cell of the region counts.
A character missing at the end of an expected row stands for a blank, and
trailing blanks in an expected row are ignored; leading blanks are not.
A wide character is written once and covers two cells; a combining mark
belongs to the character before it.
Attributes and colours are compared separately, with
.BR pty_region_compare_style (3).
.PP
.B PTY_REGION
builds a region of
.I h
rows and
.I w
columns at row
.IR r ,
column
.IR c ;
a size of 0 extends it to the screen edge.
The other fields can be set on a variable:
.TP
.I label
names the region in the report ("status bar", "menu items").
.TP
.I wildcard
is a character that matches any one cell, for content a test cannot
know, such as a clock: with
.I wildcard
set to "?", the expected text "??:??" matches any time.
.TP
.I context
is the number of unchanged rows shown around each difference in the
report; 0 means the default of 2, a negative value means none.
.SH THE REPORT
When
.I report
is not NULL it receives a description of the difference, written for
someone who did not write the test and sees it in a CI log.
It is empty on a match, always NUL-terminated, and truncated at a
character boundary to
.IR report_size .
It has three parts.
.PP
A header names the area, with its label and its geometry in screen
coordinates, and how many of its rows differ.
.PP
A diff-style view follows: a column ruler (a digit at every tenth
.I screen
column, \(oq+\(cq at every fifth); each differing row twice, as
expected (\(oq\-\(cq) and as shown (\(oq+\(cq), framed with \(oq|\(cq so
leading and trailing blanks are visible; a line of \(oq^\(cq under every
differing column; and the unchanged rows around them for orientation,
\(oq...\(cq marking rows left out.
.PP
Notes then say, for each differing row, which columns differ, what kind
of difference it is when one of these applies,
.IP \(bu 2
nothing is shown on the row, or a blank row was expected;
.IP \(bu 2
the expected text is on another row (up to three rows away, inside or
outside the region), and whether all rows moved together as a block;
.IP \(bu 2
the whole row, or the differing stretch of it, is shifted left or right
by up to four columns;
.IP \(bu 2
the text is truncated, or has extra text after the expected end;
.IP \(bu 2
only letter case differs;
.IP \(bu 2
a different kind of blank (U+00A0, U+2007, U+3000, ...) looks like a
space;
.PP
and, always, the first differing cell with its code points, which is how
a decomposed accent, a look-alike letter or a no-break space becomes
visible.
.PP
An example:
.PP
.EX
screen (24x8): text differs in 2 of 8 rows
  (\- expected, + actual, ^ differing column; ruler = screen column)
        0\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-1\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-2\-\-\-
     0 |  ┌ Inbox ──┐           |
\-    1 |  │>  Meeting           |
+    1 |  │> Meeting            |
             ^^ ^^^^^
     2 |  │  Invoice            |
     3 |                        |
\-    4 |status: ok  ??:??       |
+    4 |status: OK  12:03       |
                ^^
     5 |                        |
notes:
  row 1: cols 5\-6, 8\-12 differ
    the text in cols 5\-12 is shifted left by 1 column
    first difference at col 5: expected \(aq \(aq U+0020, actual \(aqM\(aq U+004D
  row 4: cols 8\-9 differ
    differs only in letter case
    first difference at col 8: expected \(aqo\(aq U+006F, actual \(aqO\(aq U+004F
.EE
.SH RETURN VALUE
0 if the region matches, otherwise the number of differing rows.
\-1 if the region lies (partly) outside the screen, if
.I expected
has more rows than the region or a row wider than it, or if an argument
is NULL; the report then says which.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PTYTEST_SNAPSHOT
When set, the assertion macros of
.BR pty_assert (3)
also print the actual region as a C literal after a failed comparison
(see
.BR pty_region_dump_c (3)).
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
PtyRegion status = PTY_REGION(23, 0, 1, 0);   /* last row, full width */
status.label = "status bar";
status.wildcard = "?";
const char *const expected[] = { " INBOX  3 unread        ??:??", NULL };
ASSERT_REGION_EQ(s, status, expected);
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pty_region_compare_style (3),
.BR pty_screen_dump (3),
.BR pty_assert (3),
.BR libptytest (7)
